  "final_span": "the Health Care Quality and Cost Council. Partners' \"significant market power\" is \"indirectly harmful to the Massachusetts resident, but very directly harmful to the overall market that is looking to be competitive.\"\n\"Whether or not it enables them to pay doctors a lot of money has never been reported by them,\" she said. \"But it must enable them to pay more, otherwise they wouldn't be able to staff themselves like they are.\"\nCoakley is evaluating the need for more preemptive sharing of salary details from hospitals and educational institutions in medical fields that are funded by taxpayers.\nGlobe Spotlight Team reporters Andrew C. Revkin and Mark Pratt provided coverage for this report. Ben Kesling can be reached at bkesling@wsj.com\nGlass Hospital: The Partners HealthCare spinoff fits into the trilogy of issues - -The Boston Globe, July 7, 2013 - -The problem isn't limited to kidnaps, rapes, and tortures. Governor Patrick's administration, through the release of the Commonwealth Report on partner pay disparities at other hospitals, has brought to light issues of competition breakdown in Massachusetts.\nShame on the Partners: Mass. Merck immediately lost control once it spoke, exhibiting astonishing haughtiness in relation to brain drain discussions, as noted by Bob Gebelhoff on August 5, 2013. It attempted to \"retrain\" the public relations fallout. But it failed to share crucial details about its Monsanto acquisition in a timely and transparent manner, not quickly enough.\nWhen government inspections fall short of public safety standards, they function no differently from any consumer product in terms of defecting, August 4, 2013. As you may know if you follow the popular Morgue Box space, allegedly occupied by Andy Meserghis and Miles Fernandez. Allegedly, two electroencephalography students face each other in a college room containing a viewing box with various anatomical specimens, and gaze at them.\n- - - - - - - - Image of the Day, August 4,",
